WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.667 --> 00:00:02.435 line:0%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:04.971 line:0%
-Hello. How do, Padraic?

00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:06.639 line:0%
-Sit somewhere else.

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.141 line:0%
-Huh?

00:00:11.378 --> 00:00:14.347 line:0%
Uh, but I have me pint there,
Colm.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:15.781 line:0%
-He has his pint there, Colm,

00:00:15.781 --> 00:00:18.117 line:0%
from when he came in and ordered
his pint before.

00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:21.121 line:0%
-Well, okay, I'll sit
somewhere else.

00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:33.166 line:0%
-Are you rowing?
-I didn't think we were rowing.

00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:34.967 line:0%
-Well, you are rowing.
-Well, you are rowing.

00:00:34.967 --> 00:00:37.137 line:0%
He's sitting outside on his own
like a whatchamacallit.

00:00:37.137 --> 00:00:39.139 line:0%
-It does look like we're rowing.

00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:45.011
I suppose I best go
talk to him, so...

00:00:45.011 --> 00:00:47.046
See what all this is
feckin' about.

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:48.881
-That would be the best thing.

00:00:48.881 --> 00:00:50.617
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:50.617 --> 00:00:53.419
That was a clip from
"The Banshees of Inisherin."

00:00:53.419 --> 00:00:54.720
We're here with two of the best,

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.123
Brendan Gleeson
and Colin Farrell.

00:00:57.123 --> 00:01:00.493
A really lovely depiction --
even though, obviously

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:02.628
a great deal of conflict --
of how cozy,

00:01:02.628 --> 00:01:05.497
how warm pub life can be.

00:01:05.497 --> 00:01:09.936
You obviously shot inside
for a great deal of this film.

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:11.904
And you've seen -- obviously
there's a great deal

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:13.406
of drinking,
as we see in the clip.

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:15.307
But that's not
the real Guinness.

00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:17.076
-Guinness Zero,
thank God for it.

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:19.646
-Okay.
Why thank God for Guinness Zero?

00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:21.213
-Couple of reasons.
On the production, we would have

00:01:21.213 --> 00:01:26.018
had to drink just an inutterable
amount of [bleep].

00:01:26.018 --> 00:01:27.920
[ Laughter ]
And it's not [bleep].

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:29.722
It's actually really tasty,
the Guinness Zero.

00:01:29.722 --> 00:01:32.258
It's a bit sweet for his tongue
because likes the proper stuff.

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:33.559
-Yeah.

00:01:33.559 --> 00:01:35.961
-But they only come out with it,
and it's delicious.

00:01:35.961 --> 00:01:38.331
And I've given it to a few other
people who are, you know,

00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:40.833
stout aficionados who are like,
"Jesus, that's an eight."

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:43.569
[ Laughter ]
"That's an eight, you know?"

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:46.573
So I loved it.
And plus, when I got to go home

00:01:46.573 --> 00:01:49.008
to Dublin the last time, I got
to have a few pints with me mate

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:51.610
Rue, I was telling Brendan,
and it was first time in about

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:55.548
16 years I got to sit in pub for
four hours and have eight pints.

00:01:55.548 --> 00:01:57.984
-Yeah.
-Of zero alcohol.

00:01:57.984 --> 00:01:59.953
-Yeah.
The zero's the important part.

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:01.621
-Just to have the pint there

00:02:01.621 --> 00:02:02.988
and just have the chat,
it was wonderful.

00:02:02.988 --> 00:02:05.024
-And also, on film sets,

00:02:05.024 --> 00:02:06.959
you always have to drink
the fake stuff.

00:02:06.959 --> 00:02:09.362
What were they before Guinness?

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:13.165
-Everything was ghastly. It was
actual cream and a cup of --

00:02:13.165 --> 00:02:16.135
-Grape juice with cream.
[ Audience oohs ]

00:02:16.135 --> 00:02:17.737
All sorts of drink you shouldn't
be mixing, you know.

00:02:17.737 --> 00:02:20.406
-Flat Coke and stuff like that
with a bunch of --

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:22.641
all sorts of unspeakables on top
of it.

00:02:22.641 --> 00:02:24.978
So it was okay,
but if somebody --

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.880
you know, if you had
a lot of takes,

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:31.417
and you had to drink in it,
it was just -- I mean.

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.219
[ Speaking foreign language ]
They say.

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:35.521
"It's important
to suffer for art."

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:37.456
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.456 --> 00:02:39.825
And this was suffering.

00:02:39.825 --> 00:02:41.527
-It did seem like -- you know,

00:02:41.527 --> 00:02:45.097
this takes place in the '20s,
very close quarters.

00:02:45.097 --> 00:02:48.601
The houses that you were in
seemed cramped.

00:02:48.601 --> 00:02:51.938
But is that what -- I mean, did
you travel when you were young?

00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:54.173
Did you go on vacation to places
like these islands?

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:58.077
-Yeah, well, I have been going
down there for a long time.

00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:00.880
And I think you spent --
-I spent a little bit of time

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.915
on the island we shot on,
Inishmore, when I was a young --

00:03:02.915 --> 00:03:05.017
when I was 17 or 18, we went
there for a week on the piss

00:03:05.017 --> 00:03:06.386
but I can't remember any of it.

00:03:06.386 --> 00:03:07.920
[ Laughter ]

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.089
-I couldn't remember Achill
because there was a lot of mist

00:03:10.089 --> 00:03:11.958
and rain on the days
I was there.

00:03:11.958 --> 00:03:13.960
So it was fantastic, actually,

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:16.629
just to go and see it in
all its magnificence.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:20.065
-But I guess -- So, this is
your home in the film.

00:03:20.065 --> 00:03:21.768
-On the most beautiful beach.
-Gorgeous.

00:03:21.768 --> 00:03:23.402
-Extraordinary.
Keem Bay, wasn't it?

00:03:23.402 --> 00:03:25.271
-Yeah, you're looking wrong way.
Out that way is --

00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:27.973
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-That way looks out this way.

00:03:27.973 --> 00:03:29.175
The whole Atlantic is there,
it's beautiful.

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:30.610
-That's just where Morris bit
me, actually.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.177
-That's exactly where
Morris bit him.

00:03:32.177 --> 00:03:34.314
-Is this very triggering?
Is this triggering every time?

00:03:34.314 --> 00:03:38.217
-Ow, ow! It's phantom pain.
Phantom pain.

00:03:38.217 --> 00:03:41.654
-We can't find any still from
the film you're not having just

00:03:41.654 --> 00:03:44.389
about to be bit
or having just been bit.

00:03:44.389 --> 00:03:47.593
-I need a tetanus now.

00:03:47.593 --> 00:03:49.428
-There's a bit of, you know,

00:03:49.428 --> 00:03:53.065
the fantastical and folklore
in the very title.

00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:55.101
You know, banshees,
plays into the film.

00:03:55.101 --> 00:03:59.972
Was that something -- when you
were growing up, was the sort of

00:03:59.972 --> 00:04:03.676
the fantasy element a part of
your lives at all?

00:04:05.077 --> 00:04:07.847
-In a kind of a way.
I was -- We were both city boys,

00:04:07.847 --> 00:04:09.482
so it wasn't quite as prevalent
in there.

00:04:09.482 --> 00:04:14.087
But there was a thing called
the boogeyman,

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:16.355
which I think you have a version
of over here.

00:04:16.355 --> 00:04:17.957
-Yeah.
-And you kind of tell each other

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:19.992
ghost stories and kind of
frighten the life out of

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:22.895
everybody, and they would
always -- the older people would

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:25.231
tell it to you before you go to
bed, so you had to like --

00:04:25.231 --> 00:04:27.600
So I always thought it was great
fun because it was --

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:30.770
you could get really scared but
you kind of, you know, you maybe

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:33.472
would take a sly look around
underneath the bed or wherever,

00:04:33.472 --> 00:04:35.140
but it never really
frightened us.

00:04:35.140 --> 00:04:38.344
But I had to babysit a young
German child

00:04:38.344 --> 00:04:41.314
at one point that went out --
-Good line.

00:04:41.314 --> 00:04:45.318
[ Laughter ]

00:04:45.318 --> 00:04:48.754
-I had never babysat
anybody or anything before that,

00:04:48.754 --> 00:04:51.757
and I went in, and I thought --

00:04:51.757 --> 00:04:53.959
I think this kid
was about 4 or 5.

00:04:53.959 --> 00:04:56.362
And I told him about the story
about the boogeyman

00:04:56.362 --> 00:05:00.099
and he was traumatized,
essentially.

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:02.001
-Yeah, 'cause you're
a very convincing actor.

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:03.670
[ Laughter ]

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.838
-The parents came back and they
said, "What have you done?

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:07.640
What have you done
to our child?"

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.275
He was in there screaming.
So yeah, there was always

00:05:10.275 --> 00:05:11.510
a little bit of crossover

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:13.412
between reality
and the fictional.

00:05:13.412 --> 00:05:15.948
-Yeah.
-Even in those days.

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:17.383
-You mentioned you guys are both
from Dublin.

00:05:17.383 --> 00:05:19.551
I went over with my family
in August.

00:05:19.551 --> 00:05:22.121
-Did ya?
-For a college football game.

00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:24.390
My university played over there.

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:25.658
-Your university played?

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.727
Because I know Navy and the Army
play there usually.

00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:29.862
-Yeah, so we had -- it was
Northwestern-Nebraska

00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:31.830
over there in that --
-Was it --

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:33.466
-The beautiful football stadium,
the new one.

00:05:33.466 --> 00:05:35.001
-Aviva?
-Yeah, Aviva.

00:05:35.001 --> 00:05:37.169
And it was great, but --
-Did people turn out?

00:05:37.169 --> 00:05:38.537
-People turned out, yeah.

00:05:38.537 --> 00:05:40.773
I wouldn't say it was full,
full, but it was alright.

00:05:40.773 --> 00:05:43.675
Oh, a very -- a lovely thing
happened, which was they --

00:05:43.675 --> 00:05:47.079
the registers weren't working.
There was no way to take money.

00:05:47.079 --> 00:05:49.548
-Ah, shut up, now!
The registers work in Ireland!

00:05:49.548 --> 00:05:50.515
[Bleep]!

00:05:50.515 --> 00:05:55.221
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:05:55.221 --> 00:05:57.256
-Come to Ireland where
the registers don't work.

00:05:57.256 --> 00:05:59.291
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.291 --> 00:06:01.260
-He would've turned on the news
and the televisions

00:06:01.260 --> 00:06:03.229
and see what was up,
but there was no television.

00:06:03.229 --> 00:06:05.397
[ Laughter ]
[Bleep].

00:06:05.397 --> 00:06:07.834
-It was great. The whole time
I felt like I was in the '20s.

00:06:07.834 --> 00:06:10.035
[ Laughter ]

00:06:10.035 --> 00:06:13.572
-What happened though?
-No, but they -- it was like,

00:06:13.572 --> 00:06:15.708
you know, 15, 20 minutes of
everybody being really happy --

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:18.343
sorry, being unhappy because
nobody could get anything

00:06:18.343 --> 00:06:20.513
to drink, and then they just
said basically "[Bleep] it,

00:06:20.513 --> 00:06:22.315
everything is free."
[ Laughter ]

00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:23.783
Which we thought was very --

00:06:23.783 --> 00:06:25.585
a great example of
Irish hospitality.

00:06:25.585 --> 00:06:27.220
-Oh, my God, that's amazing.

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:29.455
-What did you have to do
to bust up the registers?

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:31.657
[ Laughter ]
-Exactly.

00:06:31.657 --> 00:06:33.893
-You know, these days you just
need to know one hacker.

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:35.628
That's all it is.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:35.628 --> 00:06:37.930
But we spent the whole week

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:39.831
being very charmed
by the host city.

00:06:39.831 --> 00:06:42.668
-Did you stay in Dublin or did
you head out west at all?

00:06:42.668 --> 00:06:47.039
-My brother and parents went --
They were golfing the weekend.

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:49.808
I just went in for the game,
but we had a very charming

00:06:49.808 --> 00:06:54.046
taxi driver who had the most
Dublin of lines, I think,

00:06:54.046 --> 00:06:56.148
which is, we got into the car,

00:06:56.148 --> 00:06:58.284
and my mom will ask
a lot of questions.

00:06:58.284 --> 00:07:00.118
She's somebody who
she wants to know the day.

00:07:00.118 --> 00:07:02.455
And we get in the car, and she
says, "Alright, I have a lot of

00:07:02.455 --> 00:07:04.356
questions about
what we're doing today."

00:07:04.356 --> 00:07:06.892
And the taxi driver -- mind you,
this is someone we've known

00:07:06.892 --> 00:07:08.427
all of 15 seconds -- said,

00:07:08.427 --> 00:07:10.362
"I'll tell you what we're going
to do today.

00:07:10.362 --> 00:07:13.232
One, I'm going to drop you off
at the pub, and two,

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:15.333
you're going to stop asking
so many [bleep] questions."

00:07:15.333 --> 00:07:18.003
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:07:18.003 --> 00:07:21.606
-Lovely!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:21.606 --> 00:07:24.844
-And, you know, I can't believe
we had to go all the way

00:07:24.844 --> 00:07:27.546
to Ireland to get someone
to tell my mom the thing

00:07:27.546 --> 00:07:29.115
we've been wanting to tell her.

00:07:29.115 --> 00:07:30.783
[ Laughter ]

00:07:30.783 --> 00:07:32.718
-That is because all taxi
drivers in Dublin

00:07:32.718 --> 00:07:35.053
think of themselves
as talk show hosts.

00:07:35.053 --> 00:07:38.357
Where they do the talking.

00:07:38.357 --> 00:07:40.959
-Well, it's just such
a wonderful film.

00:07:40.959 --> 00:07:43.895
I should also say, your
co-stars, Kerry and Barry --

00:07:43.895 --> 00:07:45.431
-Oh, gosh.
-They're wonderful.

00:07:45.431 --> 00:07:46.732
-Isn't Kerry extraordinary
in the film?

00:07:46.732 --> 00:07:48.267
-She's extraordinary.
I'm very excited.

00:07:48.267 --> 00:07:49.535
She's coming on here as well,

00:07:49.535 --> 00:07:51.236
and I can't wait to tell her
in person.

00:07:51.236 --> 00:07:53.405
-Ah, you'll love her.
She's brilliant.

00:07:53.405 --> 00:07:58.110
-Kerry brought tears to eyes
that you would not imagine

00:07:58.110 --> 00:07:59.879
you can bring tears to.
-Yeah.

00:07:59.879 --> 00:08:01.347
-That performance is
extraordinary, it really is.

00:08:01.347 --> 00:08:03.048
-She's gut-wrenching in it.
Yeah, she's gut-wrenching.

00:08:03.048 --> 00:08:04.784
-And funny and brilliant.
-Yeah.

00:08:04.784 --> 00:08:07.486
-Such a vivacity about her.
So it was a bit like that.

00:08:07.486 --> 00:08:09.087
Like Martin had worked with
all that cast,

00:08:09.087 --> 00:08:10.523
apart from Barry Keoghan,

00:08:10.523 --> 00:08:13.593
who is also kind of
pretty amazing in it.

00:08:13.593 --> 00:08:15.928
And he knew the people
he was dealing with.

00:08:15.928 --> 00:08:17.662
So part of the writing was
allowing them

00:08:17.662 --> 00:08:19.598
to bring what they bring.

00:08:19.598 --> 00:08:21.734
And you can kind of tell.

00:08:21.734 --> 00:08:23.402
I don't think there's
a false note in it.

00:08:23.402 --> 00:08:26.372
-There really isn't.
And I say this about no films --

00:08:26.372 --> 00:08:27.673
when it was over, I thought,

00:08:27.673 --> 00:08:29.407
"I could have spent another hour
with everybody there."

00:08:29.407 --> 00:08:31.143
So it's really great.

00:08:31.143 --> 00:08:33.746
And it's just always such a joy
to have you both here with us.

00:08:33.746 --> 00:08:36.282
Thank you so much.
-Thanks, Seth.

00:08:36.282 --> 00:08:37.916
-"The Banshees of Inisherin"

00:08:37.916 --> 00:08:40.119
will be in theaters everywhere
October 21st.

00:08:40.119 --> 00:08:41.888
We'll be right back with more
"Late Night."

